From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [PATCH v2] jails: Fetch base packages into the host's cache
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: rlrnvwpznomnnymxslvlslltypytlrvv
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:04:13 +0000

pkg -r keeps its package cache under the root it installs into, and a
clean build starts that root as an empty dataset, so every build
fetched all of the base system's packages again, about 160 MiB each
time. Pointing pkg at the host's own cache lets every build and
instance reuse what was fetched before.

Only the host's pkg reads and writes that cache. Nothing is mounted
into the root, so neither the build's chroot nor the running jail can
reach it.
---
change-id: rlrnvwpznomnnymxslvlslltypytlrvv

base-branch: b/dev@upstream

Only the base install is cached. The ports install runs under pkg -c,
which chroots before it touches a cache, so only a mount would reach
the host's; the reply to the issue thread weighs the options.

Changes in v2:
- Drop the shared nullfs-mounted cache, which let a build's chroot
  write packages that other instances would later install. pkg -r now
  uses the host's cache by -o PKG_CACHEDIR, which only the host's pkg
  touches.
- Leave the ports install uncached, for the reason above.
- Link to v1: id:1daf75213370f9166672cd1626977587ceb16fc1.1792230947.git.claude@p7x.dev

